# Two cells side by side on paper, each a blue 72x72 square backdrop with the same content on top: a red 40x40 square left of center
# and a gold circle (radius 22) right of center that overlaps the right half of the red square and the blue backdrop, drawn with blend:multiply.
# Left cell is a `layer`: the multiply sees only the layer's own red square, so the gold circle stays pure gold over the blue backdrop
# and turns dark red-brown only where it overlaps the red square.
# Right cell is a `group`: the multiply also sees the blue backdrop, so the gold circle is dark (a dark olive green) over the blue backdrop
# as well as dark where it overlaps the red square. The difference between the two circles' colors over blue is the proof.
# Bottom row, same blue 72x72 backdrops, each holding one instance of a `def`:
# Left (def with `blend:multiply` inside): the instance is isolated, so the gold circle's multiply sees only the instance's own red square. It matches the top-left layer cell: pure gold over the blue backdrop, dark red-brown only where it overlaps the red square.
# Right (`blend:multiply` on the call): the finished instance, a plain gold circle (radius 22) from a def with no blend, multiplies onto the blue backdrop, so it is the dark olive green of the top-right group cell, not pure gold.
@target structure/layer_group.png 192x192

rect 72x72 at:48,48 fill:p.blue
layer isolated at:48,48
  rect 40x40 at:-14,0 fill:p.red
  circle 22 at:12,0 fill:p.gold blend:multiply

rect 72x72 at:144,48 fill:p.blue
group shared at:144,48
  rect 40x40 at:-14,0 fill:p.red
  circle 22 at:12,0 fill:p.gold blend:multiply

def inner_multiply
  rect 40x40 at:-14,0 fill:p.red
  circle 22 at:12,0 fill:p.gold blend:multiply

def plain_disc
  circle 22 fill:p.gold

rect 72x72 at:48,144 fill:p.blue
inner_multiply at:48,144

rect 72x72 at:144,144 fill:p.blue
plain_disc at:144,144 blend:multiply
